 "capsule": "The Heian period began in 794 when Emperor Kanmu relocated the imperial capital to Heian-kyo (Kyoto), ushering in centuries of aristocratic court culture in which Chinese-derived ritsuryo bureaucracy steadily gave way to private shoen estates and provincial power. From the mid-9th century the Fujiwara clan dominated the throne as hereditary regents (sessho) and chancellors (kampaku), marrying daughters into the imperial line and peaking under Fujiwara no Michinaga (995-1027); after 1086 retired emperors ruled from cloistered government (insei). Heian Japan produced the classical high point of court literature, including Murasaki Shikibu's Tale of Genji, before the Genpei War (1180-1185) destroyed Taira power and opened the way for Minamoto no Yoritomo's warrior government.",
 "citations": [
